# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env (it is in .gitignore).
# Connection settings and resource IDs come from your SmartSearch AI administrator.
# Values shown are placeholders: replace them with the ones you are given.
# This file works with the Java/Python/REST shell launchers and Node's --env-file loader.
# Use KEY=value assignments. Single-quote values containing spaces, $, # or shell
# metacharacters, for example SMARTSEARCH_CLIENT_SECRET='your-secret-value'.

# --- Connection settings ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Java, Python and TypeScript helpers require all six settings below, including the
# Admin URL for search-only examples. REST checks settings needed by the chosen task:
# API for search/Workplace, Admin for provisioning, auth settings for token acquisition.

# SmartSearch API gateway. Project search and Workplace (search, answers, chat) go here.
#   Project search: POST {API}/core/projects/{projectId}/search
#   Workplace:      POST {API}/workspace/v1/workspaces/{workspaceId}/search | query | chat
SMARTSEARCH_API_BASE_URL=https://api.your-company.example.com

# Search Admin. User provisioning (registering users, giving access) goes here.
#   POST {ADMIN}/search-admin/api/provisioning/v1/jobs
SMARTSEARCH_ADMIN_BASE_URL=https://admin.your-company.example.com

# Identity server base URL: omit the /realms/... suffix.
# Keep any deployment context prefix, such as /auth, if your administrator includes it.
SMARTSEARCH_AUTH_BASE_URL=https://auth.your-company.example.com

# Your realm: the name of your login space on the identity server.
# With the URL above, the client gets its tokens from:
#   https://auth.your-company.example.com/realms/your-realm/protocol/openid-connect/token
SMARTSEARCH_REALM=your-realm

# --- Your service key (your application's identity; issued in the Admin UI) ------------

SMARTSEARCH_CLIENT_ID=svc-your-key-id
SMARTSEARCH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-service-key-secret

# --- What your integration uses ---------------------------------------------------------

# Set only the IDs needed by the example you choose; unused IDs can be left blank.
# Project search: the project to search (your key must be assigned to it).
# Workplace examples use WORKSPACE_ID instead; do not substitute a managed data project.
SMARTSEARCH_PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
# Required only by SearchThroughUseCase / search_through_use_case.
# Use a use case of a project your key is assigned to.
SMARTSEARCH_USECASE_ID=your-use-case-id

# Workplace: the workspace to search (your key must be a member).
SMARTSEARCH_WORKSPACE_ID=your-workspace-id
# Optional for general search/query/chat. Required by FilterWorkplaceBySourceAndTitle,
# QueryWithFilters and RegisterUsersWithWorkspaceAccess (snake_case names in Python/TS/REST).
# Use a source in SMARTSEARCH_WORKSPACE_ID that the caller is allowed to access.
SMARTSEARCH_SOURCE_ID=your-source-id

# Required only for user registration; ordinary project/Workplace searches do not use these.
# Enable Register users on the saved service account in the Admin UI.
# Copy its Integration ID: the saved policy controlling this key's registration and grants.
# Tenant ID identifies your organisation's account, not its display name. The service key,
# registered users and allowed workspaces must belong to this same tenant.
SMARTSEARCH_INTEGRATION_ID=your-integration-id
SMARTSEARCH_TENANT_ID=your-tenant-id
# Optional: the group granted on SMARTSEARCH_SOURCE_ID by RegisterUsersWithWorkspaceAccess (default: everyone).
SMARTSEARCH_GRANT_GROUP=everyone

# --- Per-user credentials (short-lived) --------------------------------------------------
# Set these in your shell just before running; never store them in a file.
# SMARTSEARCH_USER_ACCESS_TOKEN=   SearchWorkplaceAsUser: the user's SmartSearch AI access token
# SMARTSEARCH_USER_ASSERTION=      SignInWithYourIdentityProvider: a fresh signed user assertion
#                                for the JWT grant, distinct from the SmartSearch access token above.

# --- Creating a signed user assertion (optional teaching example) -----------------------
# CreateUserAssertion / create_user_assertion also reads AUTH_BASE_URL and REALM above.
# Optional issuer and public key ID; these defaults are for the offline demonstration.
# For real sign-in, use your registered identity provider's issuer and matching JWKS key ID.
# SMARTSEARCH_ASSERTION_ISSUER=https://login.your-company.example.com
# SMARTSEARCH_ASSERTION_KEY_ID=example-key-1
# Supply SMARTSEARCH_ASSERTION_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM securely in the process environment.
# It must contain a PKCS#8 RSA private key (2048 bits or greater), not a file path.
# Do not put the private key here. Without it, the example generates a temporary demo key;
# that key cannot sign in unless its public key is trusted by your identity provider setup.

# --- REST example timeouts (optional; seconds) ------------------------------------------
# Defaults are 60 seconds for each API request/stream and for provisioning-job polling.
# Request timeout: 1-3600. Job polling timeout: 0-3600 (0 checks the job once).
# A polling timeout does not cancel the server job; continue checking that same job ID.
# SMARTSEARCH_REST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# SMARTSEARCH_REST_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# To choose another env file, set SMARTSEARCH_REST_ENV_FILE in your shell BEFORE running
# examples/rest/run.sh. Set it to /dev/null to use only your exported environment settings.
